---
import './benefits.css';
---
<figure class="benefit-figure">
  <div class="figure-heading"><span class="figure-number">Figure 1</span>What the stored answer lets us skip</div>
  <div class="scene">
    <strong>No application cache</strong>
    <div class="flow"><div class="box">Read P7 from source</div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="box">Assemble product card</div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="box">Send response</div></div>
    <strong>A hit for a cached source record</strong>
    <div class="flow"><div class="box used">Read cached record<span>Source call skipped</span></div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="box">Assemble product card</div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="box">Send response</div></div>
    <strong>A hit for an assembled product card</strong>
    <div class="flow"><div class="box used">Read cached card<span>Source call and assembly skipped</span></div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="box saved">Already assembled<span>Blue mug · GBP 20.00</span></div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="box">Send response</div></div>
    <p class="callout">Storing the card moves the reuse point further along the request. Reading and checking the cached value still takes work, and the response still has to reach the caller.</p>
  </div>
  <figcaption>The same public card for shop north, P7, en-GB and GBP. Boxes show work, not elapsed time. Both hit paths must check that the stored answer is usable for this request.</figcaption>
</figure>
